 ### Jittered Dithering Distributions
-An algorithm for quickly generating a pseudo-uniform distribution of points based on a regular 2D grid, originally proposed by Mitchell [S2, unofficial]. Create a regular 2D grid with sixteen times as many grid cells as the desired number of points. Visit each cell in turn in a left-to-right and top-to-bottom fashion. Calculate potential $D_{i,j}$ using the function: $D_{i,j} = (4D_{i-1,j} + D_{i-1,j-1} + 2D_{i,j-1} + D_{i+1,j-1}) / 8 + R$, where $R = \text{random}()$ [S2, unofficial]. If $D_{i,j}$ is greater than the threshold of one-half, select a jittered point at cell $(i,j)$ and reduce the $D_{i,j}$ potential by one. Thus, about 1/16th of the data points are selected [S2, unofficial]. Modifying the threshold or random increment to generate weighted point densities produces regular patterns in the splats and causes the number of dots deposited to vary widely from the expected number [S2, unofficial].
